// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// logic — propositional primitives over `Prop` (D63 §8.3 Phase 0).
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The `Prop` combinators the DCG determiner/connective semantics need but the
// kernel doesn't provide built-in. A small, reusable layer: depends only on
// core (`Prop` = `Sort 0`). The semantics built on it stay kernel-checked — each
// definition here is a closed `Prop`(-former) the validator admits on commit.
//
// `False` (the determiner `no` needs negation, `¬P := P -> logic:False`),
// `And`, and `Or` — the last two consumed by the generalized-conjunction
// combinator (D63 §8.4 Phase 3) and eliminated by the FraCaS conjunction
// inference (Phase 5).

namespace logic = "urn:eigenius:logic";

// Bottom — the empty proposition (zero constructors: no proof can be built).
// Negation is the idiom `P -> logic:False`; e.g. the determiner `no` denotes
// `λA. λV. ∀x:A. (V(x) -> logic:False)`. (Mirrors the `stats:False` idiom; this
// is its namespace-neutral home — statistics may migrate to it later.)
data logic:False : Prop { }

// Conjunction — the product of two propositions. The DCG generalized-conjunction
// combinator (D63 §8.4 Phase 3) builds `logic:And(P, Q)` as the meaning of a
// coordinated `S` (and pointwise-lifts it for `VP`/`TV` coordination).
//
// `P`, `Q` are PARAMETERS (sort-typed at `Prop`), mirroring Lean's
// `And (a b : Prop) : Prop`. Because parameters are fixed across the recursor's
// motive (only indices are abstracted), first-projection (`P ∧ Q ⊨ P`) is the
// ordinary parametric recursor — which is what makes the FraCaS conjunction
// inference (D63 §8.4 Phase 5) a plain elimination rather than needing the
// harder index-abstracting recursor.
// The constructor type leads with the parameter binders (`forall (P, Q)`), the
// kernel convention for parametrized inductives — `peel_ctor_telescope` strips
// `params.len()` leading binders to expose the constructor's own fields (`P`-proof,
// `Q`-proof). What makes `P`,`Q` parameters rather than indices is the `(P, Q)` in
// the *data header*, not the constructor shape.
data logic:And (P : Prop, Q : Prop) : Prop {
    conj : forall (P : Prop, Q : Prop) => P -> Q -> logic:And(P, Q)
}

// Disjunction — the sum of two propositions (`or`). Parameters, as for `And`.
data logic:Or (P : Prop, Q : Prop) : Prop {
    inl : forall (P : Prop, Q : Prop) => P -> logic:Or(P, Q),
    inr : forall (P : Prop, Q : Prop) => Q -> logic:Or(P, Q)
}

// ── Modality (D63 §8.9 Slice 6-aux) ──────────────────────────────────
// `Possible` (◇) / `Necessary` (□) — the meanings the modal auxiliaries produce
// ("HeLa can affect BRCA1" → Possible(affects(brca1, hela)); "… must …" → Necessary(…)).
//
// **OPAQUE** `Prop → Prop` operators (eigentt axioms), NOT a Kripke encoding: the
// kernel does not interpret them (no World type, no possible-worlds quantification),
// so an ordinary `Prop` stays a `Prop` and the verb axioms are untouched. Whether
// `Possible(P)` / `Necessary(P)` *holds* is a GROUNDING judgment (a `ChainWitness`,
// D39/D49) — felicity ≠ truth, exactly as for `ontology:is_a` / `subclass_of` and verb
// predications. Being opaque (not reduced to ⊤/⊥) is what lets a modal claim be carried
// as a hypothesis or a conditional antecedent ("if X can affect Y, then …") — the same
// reason the structural predicates are opaque.
//
// **Independent primitives** — no classical duality `◇P ↔ ¬□¬P` is baked in (the
// logic here is intuitionistic; `¬P := P -> logic:False`). **No modal LAWS** (T `□P→P`,
// 4, 5, duality) are asserted in this trusted layer: they are flavor-dependent (deontic
// `□` fails T) and are added as opt-in axioms per domain/flavor when reasoning needs
// them. The operators are flavor-agnostic (alethic / dynamic / deontic distinctions are
// grounding-supplied, harvested from use — like `is_a`'s actual membership); epistemic
// "may/might" may instead route to the epistemic GRADE (Declared) — a later refinement.
axiom logic:Possible : Prop -> Prop
axiom logic:Necessary : Prop -> Prop

// `Will` (future / prediction), `Would` (conditional / hypothetical), `Should` (weak
// deontic). The modal auxiliaries `will` / `would` / `should` produce these. They are
// NOT ◇/□: future and conditional are not alethic modality at all, and weak-deontic
// `should` is distinct from strong `Necessary` (`must`). Each is therefore its OWN
// opaque `Prop → Prop` axiom — same contract as `Possible`/`Necessary` above: the
// kernel does not interpret them, no laws are asserted here, and their meaning (and any
// relation among modals) is supplied on the **reasoning side** by the justification-logic
// institution, harvested from use. Keeping each modal a distinct primitive (rather than
// collapsing `will`/`would`/`should` onto ◇/□) is what lets that institution recover the
// flavor faithfully instead of from a lossy pre-commitment.
axiom logic:Will : Prop -> Prop
axiom logic:Would : Prop -> Prop
axiom logic:Should : Prop -> Prop

// NOTE: contrastive `but` (D62 §2d) maps to `logic:And` — verified adequate against every
// `but` in the WRN source (all are "X but (not) Y", truth-conditionally plain conjunction;
// the contrast is rhetorical, carried by explicit negation, and not part of the typed claim).
// A distinct contrast-preserving operator (`logic:But`) + kernel transparency (GH #95) remain
// the documented upgrade for if/when discourse/argumentation structure becomes load-bearing
// (see `docs/notes/d62-subordinator-design-findings.md`).
